INT. CLOSED FUNICULAR STATION - WAITING ROOM - NIGHT

Wooden benches, a dead ticket window, a brass clock stopped at ten past four. Rain ticks on a skylight.

AGNES PRUITT (60s, retired schoolteacher, buttoned to the chin) pours tea from a flask into four paper cups. DELROY CAMPBELL (40s, line mechanic in a grease-stained parka) sits on the bench by the door. WEI-LING CHOU (30s, insurance investigator, wet umbrella across her knees) studies the clock. FATHER TOBIAS (70s, rumpled cardigan under a black coat) leans on the ticket window, humming.

AGNES
Sugar, anyone?

DELROY
Just the tea.

WEI-LING
Who owns this station now, Mr. Campbell?

DELROY
The company. Nobody comes up here but me.

FATHER TOBIAS
And us. A fine thing, the four of us in a snowglobe.

AGNES
It's rain, Father.

FATHER TOBIAS
Then a rain globe.

Wei-Ling accepts a cup, sips, and smiles for the first time.

WEI-LING
It's good tea.

AGNES
I know.

Wei-Ling lowers the cup. Her eyes go to the ticket window. A faint metal glint sits behind the grille.

WEI-LING
Mr. Campbell. Is that the cash box?

Delroy and Father Tobias both look at the window. Nobody speaks. Delroy stands.

DELROY
It was empty when I locked it.

AGNES
Was it?

Delroy lunges for the window. Father Tobias steps in front of him, and the two grapple against the counter, the old priest surprisingly strong. Wei-Ling drops the umbrella and tries to pull them apart. Agnes sets down the flask, calmly, and the three of them freeze as she opens her coat to show a revolver tucked in her belt.

AGNES (CONT'D)
The tea was only for waiting.

Wei-Ling's cup slips from her hand. Delroy slumps onto the bench. The clock, for no reason at all, ticks forward one minute.
